# Real financial data. Never committed, never read by agents.
# See the development rules in README.md, Rule 2.
uploads/prod/**

# Operator-supplied redacted samples. The folder is tracked, its contents are not.
uploads/dummy/**
!uploads/dummy/.gitkeep

# Runtime data surfaces. Originals live in the content-addressed store on disk,
# not in git.
data/inbox/**
data/store/**
data/quarantine/**
data/reports/**
# A backup is the entire ledger and every original in one file. It is the last
# thing that should ever reach a remote. See docs/backups.md.
data/backups/**
data/*.db
data/*.db-journal
data/*.sqlite3
!data/inbox/README.md
!data/store/README.md
!data/quarantine/README.md

# The operating contract is published now, deliberately reversing the earlier
# call to keep it on disk.
#
# The reasoning then was that what ships is what builds and installs. But this
# repository expects contributors to run agents against it, and an agent with
# no access to the three binding rules cannot honour them — it will read
# uploads/prod/ because nothing told it not to, and paste what it finds into a
# fixture. The rules are only load-bearing if they arrive with the checkout.
#
# Reviewed for personal data before publishing: they describe the categories in
# the abstract and contain no instance of any of them. They are tracked now, so
# tests/test_no_personal_data.py scans them on every run like anything else.
.claude/
# Anchored. Unanchored, this matched every directory named `scripts` at any
# depth — including infra/scripts/, which is published infrastructure. The
# files already there were tracked before the rule existed so nothing broke,
# but a *new* one was silently skipped by `git add -A`, with no warning, and
# only surfaced as a workflow failing to find a script it referenced.
/scripts/

# Secrets and environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
# setuptools leaves these behind after `pip wheel .`
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Sanitised exports for categorisation. No dates, accounts or personal
# counterparties leave in these — but they are still a list of where this
# household shops, derived from prod, and they are regenerable in one command.
# See docs/prompt.md.
proposal*.json
residual*.json

# Model replies to the categorisation prompt. Sanitised of personal data by
# construction, but still this household's merchant list — and once consolidated
# the rules live in the ledger, which is the artifact worth keeping.
category/


# Node / UI
node_modules/
ui/pwa/dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
